-- The prose half of what src/lib/battery-price.ts already does for scraped
-- prices. That module's own header makes the argument: "a price is not a fact
-- about a battery, it is a claim about a shop on a particular day, and it
-- decays in a way voltage and capacity do not." Sentences decay the same way
-- and had nothing -- one dated claim across twelve guides, and no mechanism
-- that could notice.
--
-- Raised by the objection that stopped the cost guide before a word of it was
-- written: prices move week to week under tariffs and freight, so how does
-- anything written today stay current? Answering that honestly needed a
-- mechanism first, and the mechanism turned out to belong to all twelve
-- guides rather than to the one page that provoked it.
--
-- in_test rather than in_production: the weekly workflow has never fired on
-- its own schedule yet, and an alarm nobody has watched fire is not a proven
-- alarm. The report shape itself IS verified -- FRESHNESS_NOW=2028-03-01
-- renders all six claims as due.
--
-- Keep in sync with supabase/seed.sql (CI enforces this).

insert into public.roadmap_items
  (phase, category, title, description, status, dev_percent_complete, is_public, display_order)
values
  (1, 'onboarding', 'Guides: dated claims and a content-freshness alarm',
   'Shipped 2026-09-16 as the prerequisite for the cost guide, and it applies to every guide, not that one. THE GAP: scraped prices have been dated and expired since 2026-09-14 (src/lib/battery-price.ts, on the argument that a price is a claim about a shop on a particular day), while PROSE had nothing -- a grep across twelve guides found exactly ONE dated claim, in one-ground-system, and no mechanism anywhere that could notice a sentence had gone stale. The guides assert things that stop being true with no commit here: which NEC edition deleted 690.47(B), what a 400 W module weighs, which marine standards this site has and has not opened. THE UNIT THAT DECAYS IS THE CLAIM, NOT THE PAGE, which is the design decision worth keeping. A single reviewed-on stamp per guide either nags about physics or lets a code citation rot, and in practice it does the second: the page keeps looking freshly reviewed because somebody fixed a typo in the physics half. THREE CADENCES: policy 90/180 days (tariffs, tax law), technical 365/550 (code editions, datasheets), evergreen 550/900 (how this site''s own calculators work). Physics is not registered at all -- a review interval on a claim that cannot go stale is noise that trains people to ignore the alarm. TWO THRESHOLDS, deliberately: the first is when CI asks, the second is when the PAGE changes what it says, and the gap between them is the window to answer before a visitor is shown a caveat. A KNOWN CHANGE DATE OUTRANKS AGE: the Section 232 floor takes effect 4 December 2026, so a claim checked yesterday is still wrong the morning after -- changesOn makes a review come due then, however fresh the check. THE HONEST LIMIT, stated in the module and on the page: nothing here checks whether a claim is still TRUE. It records when a human last read the source. A green tick that means more than that would be a green tick that means nothing. THE DATES WERE NOT ALL SET TO TODAY -- each checkedOn came from git log on the guide file, so four guides start with real age and the oldest claim (NEC 690.7, 2026-08-30) is already the one nearest due. Stamping everything green on day one would have been the exact failure the module exists to prevent, committed by the module itself. SHIPPED: content-freshness.ts and guide-claims.ts (pure, 16 tests, no database or browser), ClaimStamp.tsx rendering a quiet date when current and an amber caveat when not, scripts/check-content-freshness.ts (credential-free, exit 0/1/2, FRESHNESS_NOW to see the report shape without waiting a year), and .github/workflows/content-freshness.yml -- a Monday cron that edits one standing issue and closes it itself, modelled on release-drift.yml. NOT A CI GATE, on purpose: a claim ageing past a threshold must never fail an unrelated pull request, because a red X people learn to merge through is worse than no check. SIX CLAIMS REGISTERED across four guides. Remaining: the cost guide''s policy claims arrive with that page, and panel-class-dimensions is retired rather than re-dated once panels carry their own physical fields.',
   'in_test', 90, true, 105);

-- The cost guide is why the machinery above exists, so the reasoning that
-- reshaped it belongs on this row rather than in a commit message. The page
-- gets SMALLER as a result: no live module $/W until panel_models exists.
-- Re-stated as a literal rather than a replace() call on purpose --
-- check-roadmap-migrations.ts compares migrations to seed.sql statically, and
-- a function call is exactly the shape it cannot follow.

update public.roadmap_items
  set description = 'Differentiator #1 applied to the question every beginner asks first and that the site cannot currently answer at all: as of 15 September 2026 nothing under src/ mentions price per watt, tariffs, tax credits or payback. Seeds differentiator #3 as well -- a $/W benchmark is the first aggregate the community layer can carry ("systems like yours came in at X/W"), which is why this is built as a reference page with a table rather than prose. WHAT IT CARRIES. (1) THE INSTALLED-PRICE GAP. The US pays roughly three times Australia''s installed price for identical hardware, and the cause is not the hardware. Customer acquisition alone ran 23% of a residential system''s price at $0.75/W in H1 2021, more than twice what the module cost that year; marketing, profit and labour together are around 43% of an American system, while modules are about 12% of what a homeowner pays. (2) A REAL BILL OF MATERIALS, so the number is not abstract: 20 x 450 W panels at $130, five 4-in-1 micro-inverters at $330, $2,000 of racking, wire and hardware, $500 of permits and $3,000 of labour -- a 9 kW build at roughly $1.05/W against about $3/W installed. (3) THE IMPORT PRICE FLOOR, and the finding that makes this page ours rather than a rewrite of everyone else''s tariff post. The Section 232 proclamation of 6 August 2026 sets a minimum import price of $0.38/W on modules and $0.22/W on cells, plus a 15% ad valorem duty, effective 12:01 a.m. ET on 4 December 2026. Against a median transacted price near $0.271/W that is about 40% on the module. On a full installed system it is roughly 2.7%, which is why the trade press treats it as a rounding error -- but this site''s reader buys bare pallets, where there is no $28,000 denominator to dilute it. The one audience for whom the floor is a real number is the one nobody is writing for. (4) THE 30% CREDIT IS GONE. P.L. 119-21 section 70506 terminated 26 U.S.C. 25D for expenditures made after 31 December 2025, and 25D already treats an expenditure as made when the original installation is completed -- so paying in 2025 for a 2026 install earns nothing. No cost figure anywhere on this site may assume 30% off. COST STAYS IN GUIDES: the non-goal added to CLAUDE.md alongside this item rules out a payback or $/W calculator, for the Winter-versus-Jeppesen reason the legal posture already sets out. VERIFY BEFORE PUBLISH: the Section 232 figures were confirmed 15 September 2026 against pv magazine USA and Anza. Trade remedies get amended -- re-check and re-date every number on this page at publish time. SCOPED 2026-09-16, from the objection that decided the shape of this page: prices move week to week under tariffs and freight, so what is written today is wrong next month. THE ANSWER IS THAT THE VOLATILITY IS NOT UNIFORM, and a page that mixes the layers decays at the rate of its fastest number. Three layers, three mechanisms. STRUCTURAL claims decay in years or not at all -- the 3x US-versus-Australia installed-price gap, soft costs at ~43% against modules at ~12%, and the reason a tariff that is a rounding error on an installed system is a real number for someone buying bare pallets. That is the half that makes this page ours and it needs no mechanism. CITED claims decay when their source changes: the Section 232 floor and the 25D termination are statutes, not market prices, and they carry a citation, a checked-on date and a published effective date. LIVE prices decay in days and MAY NOT BE TYPED INTO PROSE AT ALL -- they render from a dated source through src/lib/battery-price.ts, which already expires one at 45 days. WHAT THIS CHANGES ABOUT THE PAGE: the worked bill of materials is an illustration carrying the date it was priced, never the claim. The claim is that DIY roughly thirds the installed price because it deletes the soft-cost lines, and that survives a 20% move in module prices. PREREQUISITE, BUILT FIRST: the dated-claim machinery -- src/lib/content-freshness.ts, the registry in guide-claims.ts, the ClaimStamp component and content-freshness.yml. It applies to all twelve existing guides, not just this one, and writing this page without it would add the site''s most perishable prose to the pile nothing was tracking. SEQUENCING CONSEQUENCE: the live module $/W figure cannot ship in v1 because there is no panel_models table -- migrations hold only battery_models, and PANEL_PRESETS has one admitted panel. v1 is the structural layer plus stamped statute claims; the live $/W slot arrives with the panel pipeline. That is a smaller page than this row originally described, and it is the half that will not be wrong next month.'
  where title = 'Guide: what a solar system should cost';
